{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1782088489174,"sessionID":"ses_1135b5c4dffe1dMwyYjBsXJ2H8","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_3db1718756a748dfa9022441","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /private/var/folders/t6/p1v727892_qc9xqqdzq2rpym0000gn/T/retort-local-e69ugumc/retort-7918d3908580 && cat > ./check.ts <<'EOF'\nimport { loadDataset } from './src/loader.js';\nconst ds = loadDataset();\nconst teams = new Set<string>();\nfor (const m of ds.matches) {\n  if (m.competition === 'Brasileirao' && m.season === 2019) {\n    teams.add(m.homeTeam);\n  }\n}\nconsole.log([...teams].sort().join('\\n'));\nEOF\nnpx tsx ./check.ts 2>&1 | grep -i atletico; rm -f ./check.ts","description":"List 2019 Brasileirao Atletico teams"},"output":"Atletico\n","metadata":{"output":"Atletico\n","exit":0,"description":"List 2019 Brasileirao Atletico teams","truncated":false},"title":"List 2019 Brasileirao Atletico teams","time":{"start":1782088489161,"end":1782088489172}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Atletico has 76 matches (double!) — 31W 19D 26L = 76. That's because \"Atletico\" matches appear in BOTH Brasileirao_Matches.csv AND the historical novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv? No wait, the competition filter is 'Brasileirao' only. So Atletico appears 76 times in Brasileirao 2019. That means there are duplicate Atletico entries, OR \"Atletico-MG\" and \"Atletico-GO\" both normalize to \"Atletico\" (stripped suffix) and got merged!\n\nYes — that's the bug. `normalizeTeamName` strips the \"-MG\" and \"-GO\" suffix, so \"Atletico-MG\" and \"Atletico-GO\" both become \"Atletico\" and merge. This is a real data quality issue. The spec says strip state suffix for matching, but Atletico-MG vs Atletico-GO are different clubs. The state suffix here distinguishes same-name clubs.\n\nHmm. The spec says \"With state suffix: Palmeiras-SP\" and \"normalize team names for consistent matching\". But stripping the suffix merges distinct clubs. This is a genuine tension.\n\nFor correctness, I should NOT blindly strip the suffix when two different clubs share a base name. But the spec example shows \"Palmeiras-SP\" → \"Palmeiras\". For Palmeiras there's only one. For Atletico there are multiple (Atletico-MG, Atletico-GO, Atletico-PR).\n\nOptions:\n1. Keep the suffix for teams that have ambiguity (Atletico-MG, Atletico-PR, Atletico-GO) but strip for others. Complex.\n2. Strip suffix but when displaying, that's the canonical name — accept that Atletico-MG and Atletico-GO merge. Bad for correctness.\n3. Don't strip the suffix at all — keep \"Palmeiras-SP\" as is. But then \"Palmeiras\" queries won't match \"Palmeiras-SP\" unless teamMatches uses substring.\n\nActually my `teamMatches` uses substring on the normalized key. If I DON'T strip the suffix, then normalizeKey(\"Palmeiras-SP\") = \"palmeirassp\" and normalizeKey(\"Palmeiras\") = \"palmeiras\". The substring check `\"palmeirassp\".includes(\"palmeiras\")` = true. So querying \"Palmeiras\" would still match \"Palmeiras-SP\". And \"Atletico-MG\" stays distinct from \"Atletico-GO\". That's better!\n\nBut then the stored team name is \"Palmeiras-SP\" not \"Palmeiras\". The spec example output shows \"Palamiras\" without suffix. Hmm.\n\nActually, a cleaner approach: strip the suffix for display/canonical name, BUT for the Atletico case, keep distinguishing info. Actually the real Brazilian clubs: Atletico-MG (Clube Atlético Mineiro), Atletico-PR (Athletico Paranaense, actually now red Bull), Atletico-GO (Atlético Goianiense). They genuinely are different clubs sharing \"Atletico\".\n\nThe cleanest correct solution: DON'T strip the state suffix in normalizeTeamName. Keep it as the canonical name from the source. For matching, teamMatches does substring so \"Palmeiras\" query matches \"Palmeiras-SP\". This preserves distinctness for Atletico-MG/GO/PR.\n\nBut wait — then the historical Brasileirao file uses \"Equipe_mandante\" without suffix (e.g. \"Atletico-MG\"? let me check). And BR-Football uses \"home\" without suffix. And Copa do Brasil uses names with \"- RJ\" suffix sometimes. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, the cleanest approach that fixes the test: keep the suffix stripping for the common case but recognize that the 2019 champion issue is due to merging. Let me check what team names appear for 2019 Brasileirao.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_eecc05631001bRPK2moTrL54xI","sessionID":"ses_1135b5c4dffe1dMwyYjBsXJ2H8","messageID":"msg_eecbfc51e001qT0GClSXSy64Yw"}}
