{"type":"text","timestamp":1782088559085,"sessionID":"ses_1135b5c4dffe1dMwyYjBsXJ2H8","part":{"id":"prt_eecc106bc001jqJlAH62Umz9WT","messageID":"msg_eecc077c3001ErbTEujw4lL48f","sessionID":"ses_1135b5c4dffe1dMwyYjBsXJ2H8","type":"text","text":"I found a real correctness bug: `normalizeTeamName` strips state suffixes, merging distinct clubs like \"Atletico-MG\" and \"Atletico-PR\" into \"Atletico\". Let me refactor to disambiguate.","time":{"start":1782088533692,"end":1782088559084}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1782088559089,"sessionID":"ses_1135b5c4dffe1dMwyYjBsXJ2H8","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_18cfc6867d8b4d0480bc72f2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"/**\n * Name and date normalization helpers.\n *\n * The six source datasets use several different conventions for team names\n * (with/without a \"-SP\" state suffix, full club names, mixed-case accented\n * text) and for dates (ISO `YYYY-MM-DD`, Brazilian `DD/MM/YYYY`, ISO with\n * time `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS`). This module collapses those variations into\n * canonical forms so that lookups and joins across files behave consistently.\n *\n * Disambiguation: a bare base name like \"Atletico\" is shared by several\n * distinct clubs (Atletico-MG, Atletico-PR, Atletico-GO). Blindly stripping\n * the state suffix would merge their records. {@link TeamNameRegistry}\n * therefore keeps the suffix only when the base name is ambiguous across the\n * whole dataset, and strips it otherwise (so \"Palmeiras-SP\" -> \"Palameras\"\n * while \"Atletico-MG\" is preserved).\n */\n\n/** Strip a trailing \"-UF\" state suffix (e.g. \"Palmeiras-SP\" -> \"Palmeiras\"). */\nexport function stripStateSuffix(name: string): string {\n  return name.replace(/-[A-Z]{2}$/, '').trim();\n}\n\nconst ACCENT_MAP: Record<string, string> = {\n  á: 'a', à: 'a', ã: 'a', â: 'a', ä: 'a',\n  é: 'e', è: 'e', ê: 'e', ë: 'e',\n  í: 'i', ì: 'i', î: 'i', ï: 'i',\n  ó: 'o', ò: 'o', õ: 'o', ô: 'o', ö: 'o',\n  ú: 'u', ù: 'u', û: 'u', ü: 'u',\n  ç: 'c', ñ: 'n',\n};\nconst ACCENT_RE = new RegExp(`[${Object.keys(ACCENT_MAP).join('')}]`, 'g');\n\n/** Lowercase ASCII-folded form used only for fuzzy equality comparison. */\nexport function normalizeKey(name: string): string {\n  const folded = name\n    .normalize('NFD')\n    .replace(/[\\u0300-\\u036f]/g, (m) => ACCENT_MAP[m.toLowerCase()] ?? m)\n    .toLowerCase()\n    .replace(ACCENT_RE, (m) => ACCENT_MAP[m] ?? m)\n    .replace(/[^a-z0-9]/g, '')\n    .trim();\n  return folded;\n}\n\n/** Extract a trailing 2-letter state code from a team name, if present. */\nexport function extractState(name: string): string | undefined {\n  const m = name.match(/-([A-Z]{2})$/);\n  return m ? m[1] : undefined;\n}\n\n/** Trim + collapse internal whitespace; preserve accents/suffix. */\nexport function cleanTeamName(raw: string): string {\n  if (raw == null) return '';\n  return String(raw).trim().replace(/\\s+/g, ' ');\n}\n\n/**\n * Basic canonical team name: strips an *unambiguous* state suffix, trims, and\n * collapses whitespace. For dataset-wide disambiguation use\n * {@link TeamNameRegistry.canonical} instead.\n */\nexport function normalizeTeamName(raw: string): string {\n  return stripStateSuffix(cleanTeamName(raw));\n}\n\n/** True if two team names refer to the same team (accent/period/suffix-insensitive). */\nexport function teamsEqual(a: string, b: string): boolean {\n  return normalizeKey(a) === normalizeKey(b);\n}\n\n/** True if `name` matches `query` (team), supporting substring + normalization. */\nexport function teamMatches(name: string, query: string): boolean {\n  const nk = normalizeKey(name);\n  const qk = normalizeKey(query);\n  if (!qk) return false;\n  if (nk === qk) return true;\n  // Substring match catches \"Corinthians\" inside \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\"\n  // and \"Palmeiras\" inside \"Palmeiras-SP\".\n  return nk.includes(qk) || qk.includes(nk);\n}\n\n/**\n * Registry that resolves canonical team names across the whole dataset.\n *\n * Built by registering every (rawName, state) pair observed, then finalized.\n * A base name (suffix-stripped) is considered ambiguous if it appears with\n * more than one distinct state; for ambiguous base names the suffix is\n * retained, otherwise it is dropped.\n */\nexport class TeamNameRegistry {\n  private baseToStates = new Map<string, Set<string>>();\n  private finalized = false;\n\n  /** Register an observation of a team name (with optional state). */\n  register(rawName: string, state?: string): void {\n    const cleaned = cleanTeamName(rawName);\n    if (!cleaned) return;\n    const base = stripStateSuffix(cleaned);\n    const st = (state || extractState(cleaned) || '').toUpperCase();\n    let set = this.baseToStates.get(base);\n    if (!set) {\n      set = new Set<string>();\n      this.baseToStates.set(base, set);\n    }\n    if (st) set.add(st);\n  }\n\n  /** Finalize the registry (no more registrations). */\n  finalize(): void {\n    this.finalized = true;\n  }\n\n  /** Resolve the canonical name for an observed team. */\n  canonical(rawName: string, state?: string): string {\n    if (!this.finalized) this.finalize();\n    const cleaned = cleanTeamName(rawName);\n    if (!cleaned) return '';\n    const base = stripStateSuffix(cleaned);\n    const st = (state || extractState(cleaned) || '').toUpperCase();\n    const states = this.baseToStates.get(base);\n    // Keep the suffix when the base name maps to >1 distinct states.\n    if (states && states.size > 1 && st) {\n      return `${base}-${st}`;\n    }\n    return base;\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * Parse a date from any of the formats used in the datasets.\n * Returns ISO `YYYY-MM-DD` or null when unparseable.\n */\nexport function parseDate(raw: string): string | null {\n  if (!raw) return null;\n  const s = String(raw).trim();\n  if (!s) return null;\n\n  // ISO with optional time: 2012-05-19 18:30:00  or  2023-09-24\n  const iso = s.match(/^(\\d{4})-(\\d{2})-(\\d{2})/);\n  if (iso) {\n    const [, y, m, d] = iso;\n    return `${y}-${m}-${d}`;\n  }\n\n  // Brazilian DD/MM/YYYY\n  const br = s.match(/^(\\d{2})\\/(\\d{2})\\/(\\d{4})/);\n  if (br) {\n    const [, d, m, y] = br;\n    return `${y}-${m}-${d}`;\n  }\n\n  // YYYY.MM.DD (sometimes used in IDs)\n  const dotted = s.match(/^(\\d{4})\\.(\\d{2})\\.(\\d{2})/);\n  if (dotted) {\n    const [, y, m, d] = dotted;\n    return `${y}-${m}-${d}`;\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n\n/** Parse a year/season from a value that may be a number or numeric string. */\nexport function parseSeason(raw: unknown): number | undefined {\n  if (raw == null || raw === '') return undefined;\n  const n = typeof raw === 'number' ? raw : parseInt(String(raw), 10);\n  return Number.isFinite(n) ? n : undefined;\n}\n\n/** Parse a numeric goal value, tolerating \"2\", 2, \"2.0\", or empty. */\nexport function parseGoals(raw: unknown): number | null {\n  if (raw == null || raw === '') return null;\n  const n = typeof raw === 'number' ? raw : Number(String(raw).trim());\n  return Number.isFinite(n) ? n : null;\n}\n","filePath":"/private/var/folders/t6/p1v727892_qc9xqqdzq2rpym0000gn/T/retort-local-e69ugumc/retort-7918d3908580/src/normalize.ts"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/private/var/folders/t6/p1v727892_qc9xqqdzq2rpym0000gn/T/retort-local-e69ugumc/retort-7918d3908580/src/normalize.ts","exists":true,"truncated":false},"title":"src/normalize.ts","time":{"start":1782088559082,"end":1782088559087}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Confirmed: \"Atletico-MG\" and \"Atletico-PR\" both get stripped to \"Atletico\" by my normalizer, merging two different clubs. This is a real correctness bug.\n\nThe fix: preserve the state suffix to distinguish same-name clubs. But the spec says to normalize (strip suffix). The tension: For Palmeiras (only one), stripping is fine. For Atletico (multiple), stripping merges.\n\nBest solution: Don't strip the suffix in the canonical name. Keep \"Atletico-MG\", \"Palmeiras-SP\" as canonical. Then:\n- teamMatches uses substring matching, so \"Palmeiras\" query matches \"Palmeiras-SP\".\n- \"Atletico\" query would match BOTH \"Atletico-MG\" and \"Atletico-PR\" (substring) — that's acceptable for a broad query, but for standings we use exact team names so they stay distinct.\n\nBut the spec example output shows \"Palamiras\" without suffix in standings. Hmm. The spec example: \"1. Flamengo - 90 pts\". Flamengo in the data is \"Flamengo-RJ\". If I keep the suffix, standings would show \"Flamengo-RJ\". That contradicts the spec example.\n\nAlternative: strip the suffix ONLY for teams where it's unambiguous (only one state exists for that base name across the dataset), and keep it when ambiguous. This is the most correct approach but complex.\n\nActually, a simpler and very common approach in Brazilian soccer data: the state suffix is part of the canonical team identity ONLY when needed for disambiguation. Let me implement: compute, for each base name (suffix-stripped), the set of states. If only one state (or no state), use the base name. If multiple states, keep the suffix for those teams.\n\nLet me implement this in the loader. I'll do a two-pass: first collect all (baseName, state) pairs across all matches, then determine canonical names, then assign.\n\nActually this is getting complex. Let me think about what's cleanest and still passes the spec intent.\n\nApproach: Build a global map from baseName → Set<states>. For a team occurrence with baseName B and state S:\n- If B has only one state across the whole dataset (or S is empty), canonical = B.\n- If B has multiple states, canonical = `${B}-${S}` (keep suffix).\n\nThis way:\n- \"Palmeiras-SP\" → only SP → \"Palmeiras\"\n- \"Atletico-MG\" → MG, PR, GO exist → \"Atletico-MG\"\n- \"Flamengo-RJ\" → only RJ → \"Flamengo\"\n- \"America-MG\" vs \"America-RN\" → keep both\n- \"Sport-PE\" → only PE → \"Sport\"\n\nThis matches the spec example (Flamengo, Palmeiras without suffix) while keeping Atletico-MG distinct.\n\nFor files without state info (Copa do Brasil, Libertadores, BR-Football), the team name has no state. Those would map to base name with empty state. If a base name like \"Atletico\" appears without state AND also as \"Atletico-MG\"/\"Atletico-PR\" with states, then \"Atletico\" (no state) is ambiguous. Hmm. The Copa do Brasil file has \"América - MG\" format actually. Let me not over-engineer.\n\nLet me implement the disambiguation based on the global state map. I'll need to refactor the loader to do two passes. Let me restructure:\n\n1. Parse all raw rows into intermediate records with rawHome, rawAway, homeState, awayState.\n2. Build baseName → Set<state> map.\n3. For each record, compute canonical home/away using disambiguation rule.\n\nBut this requires restructuring the loader significantly. Let me make normalizeTeamName take a context (the global disambiguation map). Actually, let me make a TeamNormalizer class that's built once from all raw names, then applied.\n\nLet me refactor. I'll create a `TeamNormalizer` in normalize.ts that's constructed with a list of (rawName, state) pairs and provides `canonical(rawName, state?)`.\n\nActually, simpler: I'll do it in the loader. Let me change the approach:\n- The loader first reads ALL rows from all files into raw records (keeping raw names + states).\n- Builds the disambiguation map.\n- Then normalizes each record.\n\nLet me restructure the loader to separate raw parsing from normalization.\n\nActually, to keep it manageable, let me add a `buildNormalizer` function that scans all raw team+state pairs, and returns a `canonical(raw, state?)` function. Then each load function uses it. But the load functions currently call normalizeTeamName directly. I'd need to pass the normalizer.\n\nLet me refactor: \n- Add `TeamNameRegistry` class with `register(raw, state)` and `canonical(raw, state?)` methods, plus a `finalize()` that computes the disambiguation.\n- Loader: phase 1 — parse all raw rows, register all (raw, state) pairs. phase 2 — finalize. phase 3 — re-normalize using canonical.\n\nThis is a bigger refactor. Let me do it more simply: pre-scan all files to build the map, then load normally with the map available.\n\nLet me restructure the loader with a two-pass approach per file isn't enough since disambiguation is global. Let me make `loadDataset` do:\n1. Read all raw rows from all files (cheap — just CSV parse).\n2. Build baseName → Set<state> from all raw team names + their states.\n3. Create a canonicalize function.\n4. Convert raw rows → Match[] using canonicalize.\n\nLet me rewrite the loader to work with raw rows first. I'll refactor.\n\nActually, the simplest change that fixes correctness: change `normalizeTeamName` to NOT strip the suffix, but keep it. Then:\n- \"Palmeiras-SP\" stays \"Palmeiras-SP\".\n- teamMatches substring still lets \"Palmeiras\" query match.\n- Standings show \"Palmeiras-SP\" — slightly off from spec example but correct data.\n\nBut the spec example explicitly shows \"Flamengo\" without suffix. The success criteria: \"Handles team name variations correctly\". \n\nI think the disambiguation approach is the right one. Let me implement it cleanly. I'll refactor the loader.\n\nLet me rewrite loader.ts with a two-pass approach.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_eecc169e8001of1Qo7R9LncL7J","sessionID":"ses_1135b5c4dffe1dMwyYjBsXJ2H8","messageID":"msg_eecc077c3001ErbTEujw4lL48f"}}
